## 20061122

0.0.15: Removed any remaining debugging stuff, since I'm reasonably happy with this for a 0.0 final version.

1. Removed any remaining debugging stuff, since I'm reasonably happy with this for a 0.0 final version.


0.0.14

1. Simplified #read even further by replacing the full @columns by just the key when loading up the desired_columns variable.  (Started this in 0.0.13, but decided to tread lightly!)
2. A redundant return was removed from String#csv_split.


0.0.13

1. Simplified #read further for when there are no column names.
2. Modified #columns= to use the (i += 1) thingy.


0.0.12: Tried to simplify #read some by not having an extra case statement and by combining desired_columns and @columns some.

1. Tried to simplify #read some by not having an extra case statement and by combining desired_columns and @columns some.


0.0.11

1. String#cvs_split now only removes leading and trailing quotes, rather than removing all remaining quotes, and leaves alone any other quotes which are not involved in delimitation.
2. Removed a bit of debugging stuff.
3. Removed the columns_defined stuff from #read, since it really wasn't necessary.


0.0.10

1. I did that little (i += 1) trick in #columns.  Had to change the initial value to -1 though, of course.
2. CSVFile now copes with unspecified column names.  I've roughly doubled the size of #read however.  It might be more efficient to do the branching elsewhere than inside the the loop there too...
3. Made #first_line as idempotent as possible, insofar as it does a rewind after it grabs the first line.  Ideally it would take note of the current line number and then restore that.  I'll put that in the todo list...
4. Stopped using @file_handle.lineno, since it seemed to do nothing and substituted using #rewind and gets instead.


0.0.9

1. #columns= now accepts what I really wanted and that was a simple list, which becomes an array; without any asterisks either!?...  It still accepts hashes and arrays as well.
2. I changed all references to to_s to to_sym, but that wasn't working so I changed it back.  The keys as symbols, as per Todo#7 will have to wait!
3. I tested putting a comma into the test.csv and it worked fine.  I haven't fully tested all the different sorts of CSV, but I'm pretty sure it will work OK.  And it is *very* tolerant of different CSV formats.  Even to the extent of each line being different!  It also will cope will with variable gaps between commas.


0.0.8: #columns= now accepts hashes again.

1. #columns= now accepts hashes again.


0.0.7: #columns= now accepts an array, which is preferable since the column order is implicit in the order in which it is provided to the function, rather than so laboriously making it explicit with hashes.

1. #columns= now accepts an array, which is preferable since the column order is implicit in the order in which it is provided to the function, rather than so laboriously making it explicit with hashes.


0.0.6: Added a quote variable and a case statement to String#csv_split so as to remove Bug#3.

1. Added a quote variable and a case statement to String#csv_split so as to remove Bug#3.


0.0.5

1. Changed @headers and #headers to @columns and #columns.
2. Had to change columns in #read to desired_columns to accommodate Change#10.
3. Added columns as an attribute writer, so as when there isn't a header line, that same information can be programmatically 'dropped in'.  CSVFile should still be able to work even if there is no header line and no columns specified in this way.  I've added this as Todo#3.
4. Created String#csv_split, so as it will solve Bug#2.  I was going to create this initially as a method in CSVFile, but wanted to stay really OO by having this message be able to be sent to strings.  See Todo#2 for (possibly) a better way.
5. Swapped out the inline CSV splitting stuff in #columns and #from_csv for the new String#csv_split method.  So much cleaner!
6. Added a chomp into String#csv_split, since the last element still had the linefeed attached.
7. Forgot to change an instance of columns to desired_columns in #read!  Oh, so that's why!
8. Removed attr_writer :columns and replaced it with def columns= so as to control the internal representation of the columns instance variable better.  This is so as to cope with being able to define column hash keys using either symbols or strings.  It will also come in handy if I make the parameter to #columns= be able to be an array somehow...  See Todo#4.


0.0.4

1. Changed the split parameters throughout to use a more sophisticated regex which removes any trailing spaces after a comma and consequently removed the gsubs which makes for simpler code.
2. Added a gsub to the same splitter lines to cope with quoted CSV files.  It doesn't cope with commas between quotes however!  See Bugs#2.
3. Added a collect to the splitter in #headers, since I'm operating on the whole array here.
4. Added a variable field in to #from_csv to cope with the test for whether to apply a gsub, since some fields are empty.
5. Changed the modification of a header from compressing the name by removing spaces and instead replacing spaces with underscores.


0.0.3: Added a to_s into #from_csv, so as one can call the read method using symbols.

1. Added a to_s into #from_csv, so as one can call the read method using symbols.


0.0.2

1. I've changed the interface to read to accept an array as separate parameters rather than as a single parameter now.
2. So as to still be able to cope with the default of selecting all columns, I've altered the case statement which checks as to whether any columns have been specified (Is columns an empty array?), since Ruby disallows *-style parameters from having defaults.


